---
import WhatItWashesLayout from '../../layouts/WhatItWashesLayout.astro';
const locale = 'en' as const;
---
<WhatItWashesLayout
  locale={locale}
  canonicalPath="/what-it-washes/large-bakeware/"
  itemName="Large Bakeware"
  title="Large Bakeware Washer | Pullman Loaf Pans, Charlotte Molds, Brioche Pans"
  description="The JD-3 fits Pullman loaf pans (33 cm), charlotte molds, brioche molds, terrine molds, and other oversized professional bakeware. From $2,800 FOB."
  challenge={`Large professional bakeware — Pullman loaf pans, charlotte molds, brioche pans (the tall fluted ones), terrine molds, savarin rings, kugelhopf molds — are the items that don't fit in any standard dishwasher. They are also the items that develop the worst residue because they sit at the back of the kitchen unrinsed until end-of-day.

Pullman loaf pans (33 × 11 × 11 cm) are particularly tough — they have closed sides, often a lid, and the residue inside is impossible to reach with a sponge if the lid is on.`}
  howItHandles={`The JD-3 chamber accommodates Pullman pans (up to 33 cm long) when loaded with the lid removed. The standard rack supports them upright on their long edge so the spray reaches the interior bottom directly.

Charlotte and brioche molds load inverted (channels down) in the standard rack. Their decorative interior surfaces clean better in a dishwasher than by hand because the spray reaches the corners that fingers can't.

Terrine molds (the rectangular ones used for pâtés) load upright. Their straight walls make them easy targets for the wash pattern.`}
  recommendedSettings={`For large bakeware:

- Cycle: 90-second heavy-soil (these items typically have stubborn residue)
- Wash tank: 65°C
- Final rinse: 82°C+
- Detergent: alkaline commercial detergent
- Pullman pans: remove lid before loading; wash lid separately in the next cycle
- Brioche molds: invert (channels facing down)
- Charlotte molds: invert
- Kugelhopf molds (the dramatic fluted ones): invert
- Terrine molds: upright
- Tin-plated pans (traditional French): dry immediately after the cycle to prevent rust spot formation
- Anodized aluminum pans: standard cycle, but use balanced detergent to preserve coating`}
  faqs={[
    { q: 'My Pullman pan is the 35 cm "extra-long" size. Will it fit?', a: 'At 35 cm, it\'s borderline. The JD-3 chamber interior diagonal is approximately 85 cm so it fits diagonally. Standard orientation: too long.' },
    { q: 'Tin-plated French brioche pans — they\'ll rust if I leave them wet?', a: 'Yes — like any tin-plated item, dry promptly after the cycle. The JD-3 final rinse heats them, and they air-dry quickly when you open the hood, but don\'t leave them inside overnight.' },
    { q: 'Decorative copper madeleine molds?', a: 'Hand-wash these. Commercial dishwashers will dull the exterior copper and degrade the tin lining faster than hand-washing. Worth the manual labor.' },
    { q: 'Cast iron pans (Le Creuset, Lodge)?', a: 'Hand-wash. Commercial dishwasher cycles will strip the seasoning from cast iron, which takes weeks to rebuild.' },
  ]}
/>
